**Rule (Measured context)** · `rule:devperf:measurement` {{ require(quiet.gate_low_s > 0 and quiet.gate_high_s >= quiet.gate_low_s and loaded.gate_high_s >= quiet.gate_high_s, 'quiet and contended measurements must remain distinct') }}Verification gate log artifacts on the shared compute node over the documentation-only corpus measure quiet runs of about {{ quiet.number_forms.gate_low_s | display }} to {{ quiet.number_forms.gate_high_s | display }} seconds, tests about {{ quiet.number_forms.tests_s | display }} seconds and design correspondence about {{ quiet.number_forms.correspondence_s | display }} seconds; contended runs reach {{ loaded.number_forms.gate_high_s | display }} seconds, tests {{ loaded.number_forms.tests_high_s | display }} and correspondence {{ loaded.number_forms.correspondence_high_s | display }} seconds. Measurement sources: {{ quiet.source }}; {{ loaded.source }}. These approximate measured ranges motivate targets, not a controlled profiling baseline or proof of unavoidable cost. Obtain future measurements from (`rule:observe:metering`), retaining the current gate timers until tracing covers them. Record compiler and dependency versions, target, profiles, features, job count, worker CPU/memory limits, cache state, input sizes, service versions, observation mode and source identity in untracked retained run artifacts under .lane/benchmarks. Secrets remain typed under (`rule:observe:privacy`). A clean build has empty target artifacts and compiler-cache misses with dependencies already available offline; a warm one-file rebuild names its changed file and dependency fan-out. Neither includes an undeclared download or silently borrowed build output.

**Rule (Contention and comparison)** · `rule:devperf:comparison` {{ require(comparison.samples > 1 and comparison.relative_percent > 0 and comparison.absolute_ms > 0, 'comparison needs declared samples and practical thresholds') }}Every budget excess is reported even on a cold or contended run. The Python suite runner and the package-suite runner clear invoker declarations through one shared boundary before importing or executing fixtures and before spawning their children; the Python runner prepares its real gate isolation first. The declaration and host-receipt readers use the same DECLARATION_VARIABLES roster in ci/build_graph.py; run_ci.fixture_environment removes and restores those inputs even on failure. Gate-owned source, invocation and step accounting are not invoker declarations and remain available to the runner. A fixture declares its own plan, entry, mode and any conditional members; inheriting an enclosing declaration or clearing individual variables after failures is set aside because fixture repositories do not belong to that run. The foreign-environment control poisons every roster member and runs the nested declaration fixtures and a package runner child that opens an isolated fixture workspace through this boundary. A test workspace uses its own repository and any base it explicitly declares, never the enclosing sample base. Diagnose contention from recorded concurrent jobs, CPU quota/throttling or pressure, runnable load, I/O pressure, memory pressure and lock/queue waits; a large wall alone is only suspected contention. Record unavailable indicators as unavailable. Keep cold-cache, queued, contended and quiet measurements distinct; do not raise a quiet budget to fit a noisy worker. A comparable baseline uses {{ comparison.number_forms.samples | display }} scheduled quiet samples with identical workload/configuration and declared cache state; a regression finding records a median increase exceeding both {{ comparison.number_forms.relative_percent | display }} percent and {{ comparison.number_forms.absolute_ms | display }} milliseconds. This is a measurement campaign, never an automatic retry of a slow test to obtain a passing result. Preserve every original sample. The import gate is one run; a primary or lint target compilation makes that run cold, and whole-gate excess is a cold-cache measurement on which import does not wait. The invoker records its plan in its append-only declaration ledger before starting, and passes PN_GATE_PLAN as one-run, warm-after-cold-first/second or refresh-first/second. The internal locked re-entry consumes a one-use pipe token and probes the target lock; a direct flag without that token is refused before gate work. The token prevents accidental direct calls, not impersonation by a process of the same account, which can construct a matching environment and pipe. The non-blocking probe establishes that some process holds the lock at that instant, not which process holds it or that it remains held. If the supervisor exits after the probe, its descriptor closes and exclusion ends even if the gate continues. The lock is advisory and excludes only processes that acquire it; a process of the same account can consume the pipe token first and thereby refuse the genuine re-entry. The lock descriptor stays in the supervisor, so descendant lifetime cannot retain exclusion. The gate refuses an absent or unknown plan; its first line is GATE-DECLARED, retained with the run log and in .lane/gate-declaration.json before any step. The declaration line carries PN_GATE_ENTRY, a caller-provided ledger-entry identifier shared by the log and ledger. One entry names one declared run, not a series of attempts. Only a ledgered abort naming that entry permits a restart of the same commit and plan. The abort is recorded before that restart as GATE-ABORTED entry=ENTRY at=UTC commit=COMMIT plan=PLAN before_first_step=true cause=CAUSE log=LOG, with whitespace-free field values identifying the external cause and retained aborted log. The latest ledger event for the entry must be that abort; a later GATE-PLAN consumes its restart authorization. A second restart needs its own later abort, and a completed run cannot reuse the entry. The invoker checks exact field values in the append-only ledger; the gate itself checks entry form and cannot establish ledger history. GATE-DECLARED belongs to the gate alone; the invoker uses GATE-PLAN for its ledger announcement. The plan and first-cache value are invoker assertions checked for form, not authenticated evidence of prior intent or measurement. The first member declares the pair before either outcome is known. A warm-after-cold pair has no second member if the first observed cache is warm: there is no different cache state to measure. Its second member requires PN_GATE_FIRST_CACHE=cold from that first receipt and then runs regardless of the first outcome; selecting it by success or duration would condition the sample. An unknown or unavailable first receipt admits no second member, since it establishes no different measured state. A baseline-identity change instead calls for a refreshing pair, whose second member measures reuse after a complete cold or warm first receipt regardless of its outcome, even if the first already read warm. An unknown or unavailable first receipt cannot establish the refreshing pair and admits no second member. Preserve both members where admitted, including the first excess and class; the second never substitutes for the first. A repeat selected because the first was slow or red is a retry. An invocation stopped before the first step result for a cause outside the candidate tree (the node, infrastructure or invoker), recorded in the declaration ledger before the restart, is an abort, not an outcome; restarting it resumes the same declared run with the aborted log retained. A warm-peer refusal marked unresolved leaves the incumbent intact; only subsequent compatible evidence selected by the declared peer order resolves it, never a retry selected to overturn that refusal. No age-based replacement interval is admitted without a series showing drift. Whole-gate excess uses the tiers' load classes, including load-unresolved when pressure is observed but comparison is unavailable, with cold-cache taking precedence for an observed cold primary or lint build. Each whole-gate series sample uses PN_GATE_SAMPLE=import-transition, one PN_GATE_ENTRY and a declared one-run plan. PN_IMPORT_BASE names the resolved ancestor, HEAD the series source; PN_GATE_SERIES, PN_GATE_ORDINAL, PN_GATE_SERIES_COUNT, PN_GATE_SERIES_KEY and PN_GATE_SERIES_CACHE fix the label, ordinal, comparison row's count, configuration key and observed cache class. GATE-CONFIGURATION prints the key on every gate on the parallel route, so the priming run supplies it without a separate container; the key is checked again at sample start before measured work. PN_GATE_SOURCE is set by the gate and is not an invoker input. The invoker holds a series lock binding label, base and source that its imports honour and that admits that label’s priming and characterization, with all other account batteries and containers idle. It primes all owners at the series source, then characterizes the transition once as an ordinary declared run through the base-build and source-reset sequence; that run’s primary and lint receipts determine the cache class to declare. It retains the targets and checkout and performs every sample's primary transition outside the gate wall: reset to the base, assert HEAD, run python3 -B ci/run_tests.py prepare and python3 -B ci/discipline.py clippy at that base in the gate’s own container, target and environment, then reset to the series source, assert HEAD and clean while preserving the targets and build graph. These are unmeasured base builds, with PN_GATE_SAMPLE=import and no inherited PN_BUILDGRAPH_RUN or PN_GATE_STEP; they build the primary test and combined lint configurations without running a gate or changing a reference. The base build leaves the incremental state of the base, rather than the already known series content. Removing incremental caches would overpay the import; leaving the series content in them would underpay it. Owner commands are fixture-specific, including temporary mutated source and nested workspaces, and have no generic build-only entry. Owners therefore still reset to the base inside their measured preparation window and build the source through their own tests against retained source-primed targets; this measures that owner path, including an extra checkout but possibly cheaper incremental reuse, not an import's owner rebuild for every crate-changing transition. The other shared-target configurations also remain source-primed and unobserved by the two base builds: nursery advisory, minimal, rustdoc, documents, datasets, determinism, benchmarks, observation builds and cargo-config measurement. A crate-changing transition therefore yields a lower bound for such imports, not a reference, until build-only preparation can bring every compiling configuration to the base state. Adding all build commands is deferred rather than claiming the two observed configurations cover them. After priming, fetch transfers no transition objects; lock, dependency, feature or toolchain changes can also reuse artifacts already present. A sample therefore measures its named transition and those explicit cache premises, not every import. The start receipt records modification times of changed surviving tracked files from the base-to-source tree diff; the end receipt keeps those times and both primary and lint compiled sets. The check requires equal compiled sets and file sets, and every changed-file time later than the preceding attempt's end and no later than its own start. Deleted files have no modification time; the tree diff still fixes the transition. A base equal to the source refuses. The check prints evidence_strength: empty compiled sets establish only empty equality; a deletion-only transition has no surviving-file reset witness; the first attempt has no lower time bound. These observations detect missing resets between later attempts with surviving changed files, but detect neither a skipped base build nor the priming commit. Equal compiled sets do not prove the base build occurred. They are not an authenticated execution history of the invoker. The wrapper runs series-idle start after its clean on the node host, then the container gate, then series-idle end even after a timeout or container failure, before any next clean. The host shell supplies PN_GATE_CONTAINER_LIST as a JSON argv for its account’s container listing, whose stdout is a JSON array of active containers; that command, status, stdout and stderr are retained, and the host Python requires the standard tomllib module. Both idle receipts establish only this account’s observations, not node-wide idleness. A failed end listing is recorded, not discarded. The host also supplies PN_GATE_SERVICE_LIST as a nonempty JSON argv listing all running user service units of this account; stdout is a JSON array of objects with a unit string ending in .service, as emitted by a service manager’s JSON listing. The receipt retains service_command and services with status, stdout and stderr. The check reads process, container and service lists at both start and end with the same refusal rule. Only the process collector’s ancestor chain and the session-bus units dbus.service and dbus-broker.service are exempt; every other running service conservatively refuses, including a supervisor between child commands. A service-supervised sampler therefore needs an idle observation outside that service, rather than exempting its whole unit. Missing, malformed or unavailable listings refuse. The two observations cannot detect a battery wholly between them, and checking only the end would miss a battery overlapping the start. The wrapper closes each ended attempt with python3 -B ci/build_graph.py series-close STATUS (or series-close STATUS --archive-root ENTRY_DIRECTORY after copying the raw receipt files there), preserves its series-attempt.json and every receipt and sidecar in a new entry-named directory under a label-specific archive outside the checkout, and ledgers that status before proceeding. It never overwrites or selects an attempt. LEDGER is the label’s ordered JSONL outcome file beside the archive: each row has series and entry strings, ordinal as a positive integer, separate integer gate_status, close_status and archive_status, and declared_identity with exactly base, source, configuration_key and cache strings. The invoker writes the same identity on every row, from the label’s declaration before attempts run; base and source name distinct resolved commits, configuration_key is the priming key of sixty-four lowercase hexadecimal digits, and cache is warm or cold from characterization. Every row must agree; every attempt is checked against this identity before it can anchor the label. A first self-consistent but foreign receipt cannot redefine the declaration. Entry and ordinal join the directory and receipt, gate_status equals the terminal exit_status, and close_status and archive_status must be zero; missing directories, extra directories, duplicate entries, foreign labels and order disagreements refuse. A failed close or archive must be repaired from preserved evidence, not concealed as an admitted attempt. Repairing the outcome row does not preserve its former failure there: the append-only invoker history and original round log retain that history. Agreement is between invoker records. An optional PLAN_LINES JSONL file independently retains each plan as series, entry, ordinal (integer) and start (finite epoch seconds). The check with --plans PLAN_LINES refuses a planned entry absent from the ledger/archive join and a matching entry with a different ordinal or a start later than the attempt. Omission from all three remains invisible; the optional file is not an authenticated history. The plan timestamp precedes the observed attempt start, rather than being copied from its result. The command python3 -B ci/build_graph.py series-check LABEL ARCHIVE LEDGER reads every entry directory, orders attempts by their recorded start time and refuses ambiguous times or entries. A directory without a terminal attempt receipt is incomplete and blocks a decision until the same ended attempt is closed; it is not silently replaced. A closed attempt with missing measurement, a nonzero gate exit, a red functional status, a membership refusal or a pressure refusal is excluded with its entry and reason. A later attempt at an ordinal is eligible only when every earlier attempt there was refused; an earlier admitted success cannot be displaced by a quieter later success. A replacement uses that ordinal under a new entry; the check, not the invoker, chooses. One base, source tree, key, cache class, count and one-run plan, both bound idle receipts, and retained unswept owner targets remain necessary. After comparison with the ledger’s declared identity, compiled sets and changed-file sets are anchored to the first structurally valid green attempt after its reset checks, never to a refused receipt. The declared identity does not independently characterize those sets; their equality remains the stated observation, not proof of priming. {% set series_policy = row('org.peer-network.devperf.budgets', name='gate') %}The proposed sampling policy has one carrier in the budgets input: series_pressure_multiple {{ series_policy.number_forms.series_pressure_multiple | display }}, series_absolute_pressure {{ series_policy.number_forms.series_absolute_pressure | display }}, series_attempts_per_ordinal {{ series_policy.number_forms.series_attempts_per_ordinal | display }} and series_relative_floor {{ series_policy.number_forms.series_relative_floor | display }}; their boolean series_policy_provisional standing is loader-checked and printed as policy.provisional, and is {{ "proposed" if series_policy.series_policy_provisional else "assigned" }}. These proposals await sampling confirmation, not budget changes. The logged quiet pressures span approximately 0.038 through 0.077, while the contrasted loaded observations start at 0.121. The floor is proposed at the rounded upper quiet end, below the absolute bound, so an unusually quiet first set cannot refuse ordinary quiet observations. The first complete structurally valid green set under the absolute bound fixes the median forever within that label. The relative ceiling is the greater of that floor and the multiple of the frozen median: a first set above the absolute bound cannot fix the median, and an unusually quiet set cannot lower the ceiling below the declared floor. A first set between the upper quiet range and the absolute bound can still place the relative ceiling at or above the absolute bound; the absolute bound then decides, and these receipts do not prove node-wide quiet. Replacement cannot move the median. Both the absolute bound and the frozen-median multiple apply. The relative rule alone admits uniformly loaded sets; an absolute bound alone misses relative outliers. The attempt cap bounds the process rather than retrying indefinitely toward a quieter set; GATE-SERIES-CHECK prints attempt_cap so the invoker need not duplicate a literal. Exit zero with GATE-SERIES-CHECK outcome admitted establishes membership and a ceiling equal to the maximum selected pressure. Exit four reports protocol refusal, repeat with missing ordinals, or exhausted when a missing ordinal has spent its cap; none changes a tree's verdict. An exhausted label ends. A further campaign requires a new label with the cause of exhaustion recorded; it carries over no attempts, median or selections. The checker schedules nothing and changes no policy value or reference. The invoker retains every attempt, including incomplete and excluded attempts, and the first complete set that fixes the median. The whole_gate_reference_stall value is sized from the maximum admitted complete set, not from the isolated-suite ceiling; preserve every pressure, identity and raw comparison record so margins can be sized on matched observations. A separately executed suite calibration sample requires node-wide CPU some-pressure at or below {{ row('org.peer-network.devperf.budgets', name='gate').number_forms.load_unresolved_pressure_percent | display }} percent over that isolated suite's whole measured interval, the existing calibration admission ceiling; missing pressure refuses admission. This is distinct from passive-boundary baseline admission, whose before and after windows use the same ceiling while whole-gate pressure ranks the candidates. No budget or threshold is re-derived from one run. Incompatible or missing baselines leave the performance comparison inconclusive while the functional verdict remains unchanged; current budget excess still produces its finding. Repeated comparable observations strengthen attribution, not gate authority. Reviewed baseline replacement states the workload/configuration reason and never erases old findings. The mandatory lint collector and nursery advisory collector consume one invocation with warnings denied and the nursery group force-warned. The pinned compiler witness requires a nursery diagnostic to remain a warning and an ordinary warning to fail: combining the arguments preserves mandatory failure and advisory reporting without alternating cache identities. The advisory count includes nursery diagnostics through item-local allow and expect attributes: force-warn overrides their diagnostic suppression, as the pinned compiler witness checks. It is not a count of unsuppressed findings from an ordinary warn pass; reviewed source annotations remain in place and do not filter this count. A locked receipt scoped to the current gate invocation shares the compiler result between collectors; it never reuses a previous run or repeats compilation receipts. One lint target replaces the two configurations. Its allocated bytes are printed after the pass, and its live profiles count inside the existing shared-target ceiling at the quiescent gate boundary. Retired split lint layouts are removed and their reclaimed bytes printed before any live incremental state or profile is trimmed; unused layouts cannot evict useful cache entries. No cache allowance is added. Fixture listeners owned by the test process bind port zero and retain the socket while reading its assigned port. PostgreSQL and Elements receive a port before launch because their current fixture interfaces accept a configured port, not an inherited listener or a bound-port receipt. Their declared disjoint ranges are PostgreSQL 22100 through 22119 and Elements RPC 22120 through 22159. The node may reserve their union from automatic client allocation; an unreadable automatic port range has an explicit fixture refusal; the launchers refuse an overlap with the node's ephemeral range unless its reservation covers it. No node setting is changed by the gate. This prevents automatic outgoing allocation from racing a daemon launch; explicit listeners can still occupy a port and are handled by the existing allocation and startup controls. An abstract Unix socket serializes allocators until daemon startup, and Unix-domain fixture listeners have no TCP port. Scheduling or cache configuration is part of remediation; do not blame code for an unproved contention cause. Machine compatibility includes the observed CPU model set, allowed CPU count, resource-reading scopes and quiet-admission recipe. Gate comparison baselines use the passive-boundary admission in (`rule:design:development-budgets`); the primary workspace no-run build and combined lint pass jointly declare cache state from current-source, current-invocation successful receipts. Any compiled artifact makes the run cold; both complete receipts with all requested artifacts fresh establish warm. Missing, stale or incomplete receipts cannot establish warmth. Isolated fixture builds retain their separate compilation accounting. No duration threshold or caller flag supplies cache state. The first compatible retained run is provisional. Every cold or unknown run remains provisional, including a second cold run: differing compilation scopes do not establish a confirmed reference. Only complete warm evidence can confirm it; promoting a cold repeat would confuse a cache label with identical work. Cold-cache precedence applies to an over-budget tier only when it contains a compiling step named by the cache state, never from a Compiling line in an isolated build log or from overlap with another tier. Each tier prints that cache state and its compiling steps. Isolated every-run compiles are printed separately with their owner and cold_target; their CPU is charged to their owner and they grant no cold precedence. Each classification prints baseline_provisional beside baseline_source, or unavailable when no reference exists. The lint list in GATE-CACHE identifies compiled artifacts by package, target name and target kind; same-named targets of different packages or kinds remain distinct. A warm candidate replaces a cold or unknown predecessor regardless of their stalls; a cold or unknown candidate never replaces a warm predecessor. A warm provisional predecessor and a warm candidate are peers under the same pairwise rule as other warm observations; the retained run is confirmed only when that comparison could have selected either run. An unassigned margin, missing stall policy or unmatched CPU stall cannot confirm it. A historical false provisional flag without a stored deciding judgment is unproved and reads provisional; its raw measurements remain usable. Warm-over-cold cache precedence remains a deciding comparison. Confirmation does not promote a loaded candidate over a provisional peer. The candidate is compared only with the incumbent se