**Amendment (Reproduction build receipt)** · `amend:devperf:reproduction-build-receipt` This design supplies the independent running-build input of (`amend:design:reproduction-declaration`); it claims no receipt implementation or executed evidence. The gate's test-build preparation step captures provenance while producing the executable, before test inventory execution, and seals a per-executable receipt only after successful compilation/linking and inventory reconciliation. The gate build driver owns this capture, not the declaration validator. It records the clean host source commit actually compiled, the nrz revision resolved from the locked dependency graph and matched to the source used, the exact compiler/toolchain artifact, target, selected profile, ordered effective code-generation arguments and sorted resolved feature names. The existing measurement context of (`rule:devperf:measurement`) is context, not by itself this correspondence. As in (`amend:devnet:deployment-provenance`), a source pin, build recipe, binary identity and answering version serve different roles; a matching banner cannot replace their join.

The build driver observes the compiler actually invoked, its own verbose identity report and resolved installation, the locked Cargo resolution, and each contributing compilation unit's effective arguments and feature configuration, including dependency feature unification, target-specific flags and build-script contributions. Capture the actual invocation through a gate-owned compiler wrapper and reconcile Cargo's machine-readable artifact inventory; the wrapper delegates unchanged and adds no package build script, dependency or manifest edge. Preserve argument order and unit association rather than flattening different unit configurations into a fictitious single command. The profile name is paired with its resolved settings and actual emitted arguments; a named profile or requested feature list alone is insufficient. A publicly resolvable compiler distribution artifact and its verified contents must agree with the compiler, sysroot and code-generation backend actually used; retain the distribution manifest, component locators and verification observations with the local receipt. A rustc commit/version or installation path alone is not that artifact. If exact artifact correspondence cannot be established, receipt capture is unavailable, never guessed from the channel. A reused Cargo output requires its previously captured producing receipt and a matching complete input/artifact inventory; a fresh cache flag alone cannot mint provenance for an earlier binary. Missing provenance requires a build with capture before reproduction can run, not reconstruction from the later environment. This changes no cache classification or performance budget.

Bind the receipt to the exact linked test executable from the build inventory and its required runtime artifacts by retained byte identities and lengths, including dynamically loaded build artifacts when present. The gate retains those artifacts in its controlled build workspace, excludes replacement during dispatch and execution, and checks the launched executable against that inventory; the test harness compares its current executable byte for byte with the retained executable that the gate checked against the receipt before accepting it. Streaming byte comparison uses the standard library and needs no hashing edge in epoch or graph. A missing artifact, changed bytes, mismatched executable, incomplete invocation inventory or malformed receipt is unavailable reproduction. Local SHA-256 byte fingerprints are computed and checked by the gate's existing Python standard library; no new dependency or manifest edge is required. These fingerprints identify exact local artifact bytes for the harness's stale-binary refusal only, are recomputable from those retained bytes, remain untracked run receipts and never enter the tree or a published record. They authenticate no process of the same account and prove no host action: the gate's controlled capture and dispatch are the trust boundary, not a self-authenticating hash. Replacing or publishing this local identity requires (`gate:identity:implementation`) and (`req:identity:admission-record`) before any tree or package value is assigned; publication and host attribution remain held by (`open:identity:reproduction-binding`).

The instrument validates the receipt and compares every declared build member with its independently captured counterpart before either execution. A differing member, absent or malformed receipt, or artifact mismatch is unavailable, not a discrepancy; guard exhaustion is incomplete. Both fail the reproduction tests. Reproduction fixtures are ordinary required tests, not silently ignored tests outside the gate: a developer running them without the producing receipt sees an explicit unavailable-reproduction failure and must use the capture-and-run path. Other certificate-verification tests need no receipt and remain runnable independently. Gate execution must include the positive completed equality and all negative receipt/member controls; a missing receipt cannot turn the mandatory test into a skip. Neither receipt presence nor any package metadata selects harness mode. The public declaration and package binding keep their separate design and identity gates. This receipt is a local test instrument, not a deployment provenance attestation.

**Rule (Build configuration)** · `rule:devperf:builds` A build or aggregate gate budget excess opens a misconfiguration finding as well as its measured warning; classify the responsible phase before changing configuration. Investigate dev/test optimisation, codegen units, debug information, link time, incremental reuse, compiler-cache hit rate, enabled features and dependency weight. Keep clean, warm and one-file-edit measurements separate, and vary one proposed choice at a time with comparable workloads. Preserve debugging utility, all required tests, supported features and the exact release semantics. A gate dominated by a necessary full-corpus check calls for that check's profiling or registry review, not an unrelated compiler flag. Missing measurements are an explicit performance-evidence hold, not a timing-derived failure. Findings remain owned until corrected with evidence or covered by an admitted narrow registry entry; contention classification alone does not close them. The six named whole-tree repository checks use their own registered process-and-reaped-children CPU budgets. The command wall remains printed separately; neither figure changes its functional verdict. A measured CPU excess emits whole-tree-cpu-growth and enters the same over-budget ledger. Missing CPU is missing evidence, never zero. The retained step context compares current and previous own CPU with source identity, without attributing a difference to code or load. Per-step budgets fit the retained measurements: the near-quiet hash audit stayed nearly flat as campaign bytes grew, while the remaining checks exercise fixture inventories rather than one uniform byte stream. A byte-scaled allowance would conceal that distinction. Each row records the measured CPU and whether it remains provisional; the provisional rows use retained loaded measurements and make no quiet-calibration claim. The warning margin follows the existing growth margin, rounded upward; quiet remeasurement can replace these provisional values. Case, tier and whole-gate budgets are unchanged.

**Decision (Measured profiles)** · `dec:devperf:profiles` The foundation bite measures Cargo's current dev and test defaults, compares candidate settings on clean and incremental builds plus the same test corpus, and records the chosen settings and tradeoffs as explicit decisions with their data. It then changes the profiles and the build-policy checker together under (`amend:toolchain:development-performance`), retaining defaults only where the measurements justify them. No setting is selected merely because it is reputed fast, and no release setting changes here. This resolves the ownership and closing evidence of record 013's open measurement item; its numerical choices remain held for that bite. No profile or build code changes in this record.

**Rule (Expensive-operation admission)** · `rule:devperf:registry` Unavoidably expensive work is admitted only through the dataset-rendered register below. Each entry mints a stable repository label and states its operation and workload, measured wall and provenance, proposed or admitted budget, irreducible work and attempted reductions, placement, owner and review status. Candidate entries track evidence still owed and grant no exception. Admission requires the owner and reviewer to accept the measured residual cost after profiling; merely exceeding a budget, being contended or calling work important is insufficient. An admitted entry changes only that operation's warning threshold and declared placement, never its verdict or evidence obligations; exceeding its own budget still warns and opens a finding. Unregistered excess is a finding, not a back door to timing-based gate failure. Larger service or benchmark runs may live only on suitable workers, but moving them out of the fast path does not waive their required evidence. Withdraw an entry when comparable measurements fit the ordinary budget, updating citations in the same change under (`rule:compatibility:pre-one`). Do not retain an obsolete expensive exception for convenience.

